text:
The map shows a mine and a settlement on the footprint, a wetland at the salar edge, and no mapped roads or Indigenous land, which probably reflects OSM coverage in the Puna. I'll run a few desk searches on the place.

**Screen: mine, Salar del Hombre Muerto area, Catamarca, Argentina (-25.46664, -67.0966)**
Footprint: 3 km radius around the point. Stage not given, so I assumed greenfield. The map shows existing lithium operations on the same salar, so the stage matters a lot and needs confirming.

**Overall:** A sensitive, water-dependent site in an arid, endorheic salt flat with contested cumulative water use. Likely Category A.

**Flags, most serious first**

1. **Wetland and water (PS6, high).** The Salar del Hombre Muerto wetland is mapped on the footprint (0 km, OSM). Desk research: Since 1997, lithium extraction by Minera del Altiplano S.A. —a subsidiary of Livent, currently owned by Rio Tinto— has caused significant environmental damage at Salar del Hombre Muerto. This includes the drying of a 5-kilometer arm of the Trapiche River wetland, an impact that persists to this day. In 2024, the Supreme Court of Catamarca recognized the damage to the Trapiche wetland and ordered the provincial government to refrain from granting new mining permits in the Los Patos River basin until a comprehensive cumulative environmental impact assessment is completed. (Desk research: FARN, https://farn.org.ar/en/communities-in-argentina-chile-and-bolivia-raise-alarm-over-lithium-mining-impacts/; AIDA, 24 September 2024.)

2. **Indigenous communities (PS7, medium).** No Indigenous land is mapped within 20 km, which is a gap rather than an absence. Desk research names the Atacameños del Altiplano community, which has a pending lawsuit. The writ was filed as part of the amparo action filed by the Atacameños del Altiplano Indigenous Community in August 2021. Catamarca has been criticised for not applying recognition and consultation laws: Catamarca and Salta denied those rights to indigenous groups. (Desk research: Springer chapter, https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-99824-9_6.)

3. **Livelihoods and economic displacement (PS5, medium).** No farmland is mapped within 1 km and no settlement within 5 km, but desk research describes livestock, farming and tourism depending on the rivers: the drying up of rivers due to mining operations makes it impossible for communities to continue with their economic activities, which are mainly the livestock industry, agriculture and tourism. Grazing is likely unmapped.

4. **Cultural heritage (PS8, medium).** No heritage features are mapped within 5 km. Desk research describes a historic gold site on the south-west shore: It was mined in the 15th century by the Lickan Antay (or Likanantaí) people. Its location relative to the footprint is unknown. A mapped "Incahuasi Mines" sits 4.27 km away (Wikidata); I have not confirmed it is the same site. Large earthworks in a long-settled area call for a heritage survey and a chance-finds procedure.

5. **Access, traffic and workforce (PS4, medium-low).** OSM shows no main road within 10 km, which is probably a coverage gap. Desk research says the route from this town or from Salta must use provincial route 43, which becomes very sandy in some sections and may be impassable. A local resident describes the town where I live in Antofagasta de la Sierra went from being a very quiet town to one with a lot of activity. The Incahuasi settlement is 6.62 km away (Wikidata).

6. **Birds and unprotected habitat (PS6, low).** The Salar is recorded as an unprotected AICA with site ID 19374 (criteria A1, A4i, last updated 2008). The area is not protected. It also temporarily concentrates waterbird species. No CR or EN GBIF records were found within 5 km or 20 km. That is not evidence of absence.

7. **Permits and cumulative-impact disputes (context, medium).** Currently, 12 lithium projects are underway in the area, at different stages of development.
